.page play 1 play WAV files and raw audio

# SYNOPSIS
*play* [*-q*] [*-v* _PERCENT_] _FILE.wav_ ...
*play* *-r* _RATE_ *-c* _1|2_ *-b* _BITS_ [*-q*] _FILE_ ...

# DESCRIPTION
`play` sends audio files to the sound card through `/dev/audio` and returns when the last one has been heard. A file that starts like a WAV file is played with the format its header gives (PCM, 8, 16, 24 or 32 bits, mono or stereo, any rate from 4000 to 192000 Hz). Anything else is taken to be raw samples: little endian, with the format named by `-r`, `-c` and `-b` (44100 Hz, stereo, 16 bits if they are left out). The file name `-` reads raw samples from standard input.

The kernel mixes `play` with every other program that is making sound, converts the rate and the sample size, and applies the master volume (see `volume(1)`). Ctrl+C stops the sound at once. If the machine has no sound device `play` says so and exits with status 1.

# OPTIONS
@ *-q*
    Do not print the format of each file.
@ *-v* _PERCENT_
    The volume of this playback alone, 0 to 100, on top of the master volume.
@ *-r* _RATE_
    Raw input: frames per second.
@ *-c* _CHANNELS_
    Raw input: 1 or 2.
@ *-b* _BITS_
    Raw input: 8 (unsigned), 16, 24 or 32 (signed).

# EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if there is no sound device or a file could not be played; 2 for a usage error.

# EXAMPLES
    play ~/Music/shrine.wav
    play -q -v 40 a.wav b.wav
    play -r 22050 -c 1 -b 8 voice.raw
    beep -f 330 -o /tmp/e4.wav && play /tmp/e4.wav

# SEE ALSO
beep(1), volume(1), audio(7)
